Z||000007||StuGood||01-24-2001||12:59 PM||||Does the radiator cooling fan ever come on?    ||||reg||5
Z||000008||Tigger||01-24-2001||01:02 PM||||Ummmm.....  if everything else is fine, thermostat checks ok, coolant is ok (I'm assume would be good), and system has been burped, then check to see if the radiator isn't plugged with calcium.<P>Easy to check, w/engine cool, open the radiator cap and look in.<P>Is the radiator old?  Those little accordian thingys in between the rad bars rust away and if enough of them get bent over, will reduce the cooling capacity.  <BR>Does the rad fan work ok?<P>Last thing I can think of would be to check the alternator belt.  If it's loose it could be slipping over the waterpump pulley and causing it to loose coolant circulation.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Greg Piet||01-24-2001||01:08 PM||||Call me wacky, but an easy check as well is to see if the timing is off.  If so it is possible that the car will run fine, but overheat.<P>That could be another problem.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA Region 72<BR>#47 CSP<BR>Bring it to the track.<BR>Lets see if all those mods really helped!||||reg||1
Z||000010||sqoach||01-24-2001||01:31 PM|||| yes, fan works. have a new fan motor and relay (about 4 months ago) i can turn on the a/c and the fan comes on. i'm not sure if the fan switch works though. but, the temp. rose so fast (2 or 3 minutes) that the fan wouldn't have made a difference.<BR> the stat is ok. i checked it out the day i bought it to make sure. coolant is good. probably not even a month old. i think the radiator has been there ever since the car was made. so i'll check that out, or have someone look at it for me. and i burped it about 2 weeks ago. after that it did fine until now. i bet if i burp it again it will do ok for a while, like it usually does.<BR> tig, how tight does the alt. belt have to be? i have it on there kinda tight. i still hear a little "squeal" on start up. maybe not tight enough?<BR> greg, how exactly do you check the timing? i've thought about that before but didn't know what to do.<P>thanks guys. hope i can get this fixed without taking it to the shop like i planned on doing.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Gold-86SE||01-24-2001||02:19 PM||||<BR>My 86 SE did that to me.  Cause of problem - water pump went south.  I suggest you replace the water pump.<BR><P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Cold and Rain Daily Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||sqoach||01-24-2001||03:09 PM||||water pump is good. replaced that about 3 months ago.<BR>i just did notice something kind of strange as i was re-filling my system. as i was adding coolant to the stat opening, the overflow tank in front was filling up. could the radiator cap be bad? i just bought it a few weeks ago. it's the kind with the little red lever on it.||||reg||1
Z||000013||ray b||01-24-2001||03:35 PM|||| ck cap is it vented parts book said to use vented cap BUT ARE WRONG use only NON-VENTED caps both caps. O-ring needed on stat use wet sandpaper to clean out rust in neck so stat will slide in . ray||||reg||1
Z||000014||Raydar||01-24-2001||04:56 PM||||I think the cap is gonna be the problem. It sounds like it's either the wrong kind, or it's bad. There is no way that just filling the system should make the overflow bottle fill up. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000015||Fformula88||01-24-2001||05:11 PM||||Try replacing the caps.  Even with burping it a couple weeks ago, air will get back in if the cap is bad.  On our two Fieros it seems that whenever they start overheating its from air in the system.  <P>------------------<BR>John<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">     <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1987 Fiero Sport Coupe *In rebuilding*<BR>1997 Ford Probe SE<BR>AOL IM: Fformula88||||reg||1
Z||000016||Carrolles||01-24-2001||05:45 PM||||As others have said, the vented cap is wrong.  You need the non-vented cap.<P>The vented cap lets coolant siphon into the overflow bottle when the car sits with the nose slightly down.  I noticed this when I had the rear end jacked way up on jack stands to pull maintenance under the engine.  Coolant trickled out of the overflow bottle until it ran out.  <P>Apparently yours has been dribbling a little and when the engine runs and the cools down it sucks air back into the coolant system.  It does not take much of an air bubble to air lock the water pump.  <P>When I last had my cooling system apart for service I followed the burping procedure when refilling.  When I was through it ran fine for a few miles then did what yours did.  Temp skyrocketed in a few seconds.  I burped it again and it only took about a cup of water to refill.  That did the trick.  That small air bubble was circulating through the system and when it hit the water pump it lost prime and quit circulating long enough for the temp to hit the roof.<P>I got my radiator cap at AutoZone.  Their book listed the vented cap.  I asked for the same cap in the non-vented version.  It works fine now.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000017||sqoach||01-24-2001||06:02 PM||||i just picked up another cap to see if the problem was the other i bought a while back. it did the same thing. overflow tank started filling up. i put my old stock one back on for now. is it vented or non? it's holding the coolant in the radiator and not allowing any to flow into the overflow tank, that's why i put it back on there. i also put my o-ring back on the stat. i got it to fit in there now.<BR>i asked for a vented cap when i got this new one, and i guess they never heard of one. probably just the guy working there at the time. is the part # different on the non-vented as opposed to the vented? can someone give me a parts number? and what stores do you go to, opposed to autozone? (not one around here) thanks everone.<P>also, forgot to ask how tight does the alt. belt need to be? and even the a/c belt? thanks.<P>oops! meant to put i asked for a non-vented cap up there.<P>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 01-24-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 01-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||StuGood||01-24-2001||06:26 PM||||Just make sure the little disc on the bottom seals off the cap.  It should be held there by light spring tension.  If you invert the cap ("knob" end up) and the disc is free to fall away, you have a vented (wrong) cap. <P>I believe you want a <B>non</B>-vented cap.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 01-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||sqoach||01-24-2001||06:43 PM||||stugood, i noticed that when i was looking at both radiator caps. the stock one would spring back when i pulled down that little disc, and the one i bought would not. i guess that answers my question of how you can tell the difference. thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000020||ray b||01-24-2001||07:30 PM|||| most caps that are vented will say vented on them . robertshaw brand say vented by 16lbs. non-vented just say 16lbs, RR-30 is part# but any brand should say vented if it is .DO NOT USE VENTED     ray||||reg||1
